templ Metal() {
	<article id="metal">
		// <h1 class="font-bold font-montserratSemibold my-4">The Best Way to Recycle Metal and Keep It in Good Condition</h1>
		// Metal is one of the most valuable materials in the recycling industry, and recycling it is essential for conserving natural resources, reducing landfill waste, and mitigating the environmental impacts of mining and processing raw metals. However, to make metal recycling effective, it's crucial to know the right steps to follow and how to prepare metal items for recycling. In this blog post, we will look at the proper ways of recycling metals.
		// <h3 class="my-2 font-semibold font-montserrat">How to Recycle Metal</h3>
		// Here's a simple guide to help you recycle metal and ensure it stays in perfect condition for proper processing.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">1. Identify Recyclable Metal</h5>
		// Not all metals are recyclable, so the first step is to identify the types of metal that can be recycled. Common recyclable metals include:
		// - Aluminum (used in soda cans, foil, and food containers)
		// - Copper (used in electrical wiring, pipes, and fixtures)
		// - Steel (used in food cans, cars, and construction materials)
		// - Tin (used in food cans and packaging materials)
		// Check with your local recycling center or platform like Bel Tech for guidelines on recyclable metals in your area.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">2. Clean the Metal Items</h5>
		// Cleaning metal items before recycling is essential to ensure they are processed correctly. Dirty metal can contaminate entire batches of recyclables, making it harder to process. This is how to clean metals properly:
		// - Rinse out food containers, cans, and other metal items to remove any residue.
		// - Use plain water and avoid leaving food scraps or liquids inside.
		// - For sticky residues, use a little soap to clean thoroughly.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">3. Remove Non-Metal Parts</h5>
		// Many metal items come with non-metal materials like plastic caps, paper labels, or rubber seals. These need to be removed before recycling. Recycling facilities are designed to process pure metal, so mixing materials can cause problems. You can separately recycle the non-metal parts if possible, or dispose of them properly.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">4. Sort Metal Items by Type</h5>
		// To ensure efficient recycling, sort metal items by type. This helps recycling facilities process the metals correctly and produces higher-quality recyclables. Here's how to sort metals:
		// - Separate ferrous metals (like steel and iron) from non-ferrous metals (like aluminum, copper, and tin).
		// - Sort metal items into categories like aluminum cans, steel food cans, and copper wiring.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">5. Store Metals Properly Before Recycling</h5>
		// To keep metals in perfect condition for recycling, store them in a clean, dry place before dropping them off at a recycling center or collection point. This helps prevent contamination by dirt or moisture, which can make the metal harder to process.
		// <p class="font-montserratSemibold font-bold my-3">Conclusion</p>
		// Recycling metal effectively requires a few simple but important steps. Identify recyclable metals, clean them thoroughly, remove non-metal parts, sort metal items by type, and store them properly. When you do this, you ensure that the metal is in the best condition for recycling.
		// Bel Tech is helping Nigerians make a difference by creating opportunities for proper waste recycling. With platforms like theirs, recycling becomes easy, rewarding, and impactful. Start recycling today and contribute to a cleaner, healthier Nigeria.
		// Want to learn more about recycling and sustainable waste management? Follow BelTech on all social media platforms for tips, updates, and opportunities to make a difference in our country.
	</article>
}

templ Plastics() {
	<article id="plastic">
		// <h1 class="font-bold font-montserratSemibold my-4">The Best Way to Recycle Plastic and Keep It in Good Condition</h1>
		// Plastic is one of the most common types of waste generated in Nigeria, and recycling it is essential for reducing pollution and conserving resources. 
		// However, to make plastic recycling effective, it’s very important to know the right steps to follow and how to prepare plastic items for recycling. 
		// In this blog post, we will look at the proper ways of recycling plastics. 
		// <h3 class="my-2 font-semibold font-montserrat">How to Recycle Metal</h3>
		// Here’s a simple guide to help you recycle plastic and ensure it stays in perfect condition for proper processing.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">1. Identify Recyclable Plastics</h5>
		// Not all plastics are recyclable, so the first step is to identify the types of plastic that can be recycled. 
		// Look for the recycling symbol (a triangle of arrows) on the plastic item. Inside the symbol, you’ll see a number that indicates the type of plastic.
		// Common recyclable plastics include; PET (Polyethylene Terephthalate) which is used in water bottles, soda bottles, and food containers and HDPE (High-Density Polyethylene) which can be found in detergent bottles, milk jugs, and shampoo containers.
		// Plastics like PVC (used in pipes) or polystyrene (Styrofoam) are harder to recycle, so check with your local recycling center or platform like Bel Tech for guidelines.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">2. Clean the Plastic Items</h5>
		// One of the most important steps in plastic recycling is to clean the items before sending them for recycling. Dirty plastic can contaminate entire batches of recyclables, making it harder to process.
		// This is how to clean plastics properly:Rinse out food containers, bottles, and jars to remove any residue.
		// Use plain water and avoid leaving food scraps or liquids inside.
		// For sticky residues, use a little soap to clean thoroughly.
		// For instance, before recycling a yoghurt or coke container, rinse it out completely and remove any attached foil or paper labels.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">3. Remove Non-Plastic Parts</h5>
		// Many plastic items come with non-plastic materials like metal caps, paper labels, or rubber seals. These need to be removed before recycling. 
		// Recycling facilities are designed to process pure plastic, so mixing materials can cause problems.
		// You can separately recycle the non-plastic parts if possible, or dispose of them properly.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">4. Flatten or Compress Plastic Items</h5>
		// To save space and make transportation easier, flatten or compress plastic items before recycling. This is especially useful for bulky plastic bottles or containers.
		// Here’s how to do it:
		// Crush plastic bottles after removing the caps.Stack flat items like trays or containers into a sack to save space.
		// Flattening also ensures that more plastic can fit into collection bins or bags, making the process more efficient.
		// <h5 class="font-bold my-2 font-montserrat">5. Store Plastics Properly Before Recycling</h5>
		// To keep plastics in perfect condition for recycling, store them in a clean, dry place before dropping them off at a recycling center or collection point. 
		// This helps prevent contamination by dirt or moisture, which can make the plastic harder to process.
		// For instance, you can use a separate bag or container specifically for recyclable plastics. Also avoid mixing plastics with other waste like food scraps or glass.
		// In Nigeria, platforms like Bel Tech provide designated collection points for plastics. You can drop off your cleaned and sorted plastics at these points to ensure they are properly recycled.
		// <p class="font-montserratSemibold font-bold my-3">Conclusion</p>
		// Recycling plastic effectively requires a few simple but important steps. Identify recyclable plastics, clean them thoroughly, remove non-plastic parts, flatten them to save space, and store them properly. 
		// When you do this, you ensure that the plastic is in the best condition for recycling.
		// Bel Tech is helping Nigerians make a difference by creating opportunities for proper waste recycling.
		// With platforms like theirs, recycling becomes easy, rewarding, and impactful. Start recycling today and contribute to a cleaner, healthier Nigeria. 
		// Want to learn more about recycling and sustainable waste management? Follow BelTech on all social media platforms for tips, updates, and opportunities to make a difference in our country.
	</article>
}
